.TH OTPME-VLAN 1 "2026-07-26" "OTPme 0.3.0a120" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-vlan \- manage OTPme VLANs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-vlan
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIvlan\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-vlan
manages VLAN objects in the OTPme system. A VLAN object is the assignable
representation of a network VLAN. Its value is returned as
.B Tunnel\-Private\-Group\-Id
during 802.1x or MAB port authentication.
.PP
There are two ways to assign a VLAN. The VLAN can be named on the object,
through the
.B vlans
config parameter of sites, units, hosts, devices, users and tokens. Or the
object can be named on the VLAN, by adding it as a member with
.BR add_token ", " add_role ", " add_host " or " add_device .
.PP
Membership is the way to keep assignments where the network is. It only ever
applies to VLANs of the site answering the RADIUS request, so a site makes
all of its own assignments without touching objects that are owned somewhere
else, and without any of the cross site rules further down: the member list
lives on the VLAN object, which only its own site can edit. Member lists are
not synced to other sites.
.PP
The more specific assignment wins. A VLAN naming the object itself comes
first, then one naming a role the object is in, then one naming a role that
role is in, and so on up the nesting. So a single token can be moved out of
the VLAN of its role, and a role nested in another can have a VLAN of its
own. All of them win over the
.B vlans
config parameter, which is only looked at when no VLAN of our site names the
object.
.PP
Where two VLANs name the object at the same distance -- it is in two roles
of the same nesting level, each in a different VLAN -- there is nothing to
tell them apart. The VLAN whose name sorts first is used, so the answer is
at least the same every time, and a warning naming both is logged.
.PP
An object can only be a member of one VLAN per site. Adding it to a second
one is refused, naming the VLAN it is already in. Only membership entered
directly counts here: a role in another VLAN is not a conflict, it is what a
more specific entry is meant to override.
.PP
The rest of this section is about the
.B vlans
config parameter. Assigning a VLAN that way requires the
.B assign
ACL on the VLAN object. This makes VLAN assignment delegable per VLAN: an
operator can be allowed to hand out the guest VLAN without being able to put
anybody into the server VLAN.
.PP
VLANs are site local objects, but they can be assigned across sites by
prefixing the site name (\fIsite\fB/\fIvlan\fR). This is needed when users
are created on the master site only while each site runs its own VLANs.
.PP
An object can have one VLAN per site assigned, because an object may be used
at more than one site. The site answering the RADIUS request returns its own
VLAN. If none of the assigned VLANs belongs to it, it returns the first one
its
.B vlan_trusts
allow, in the order of those entries.
.PP
A cross site assignment is honoured only if the site answering the RADIUS
request lists it under
.BR vlan_trusts ,
as \fIsite\fR[\fB:\fIvlan_site\fR[\fB/\fIvlan\fR]] (see
.BR otpme (7)).
The VLAN is switched in that site's network, whichever site owns the VLAN
object, so that site decides. The
.B assign
ACL alone is not enough here, because it is checked by the site making the
assignment against its own copy of the VLAN, so a site holding the users
cannot put them into a VLAN of a network that did not agree.
.PP
A VLAN object carries an optional VLAN ID. If a VLAN ID is set it is sent to
the switch. Without a VLAN ID the VLAN name is sent instead, which is what
switches configured with named VLANs (e.g. Cisco) expect.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS VLAN Management
.TP
.B add \fIvlan\fR \fR[\fIvlan_id\fR]
Create a new VLAN, optionally with a VLAN ID.
.TP
.B del \fIvlan\fR
Delete a VLAN. Asks for confirmation according to the confirmation policy, saying how many members it still has. Members lose their VLAN with it, nothing is left behind. Objects that name the VLAN in their \fBvlans\fR config parameter keep an unresolvable assignment, they are not listed because that parameter is not indexed.
.TP
.B show \fR[\fIvlan\fR]
Display VLAN information.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIregex\fR]
List VLANs, optionally filtered by regex pattern.
.TP
.B enable \fIvlan\fR
Enable a disabled VLAN. A disabled VLAN is not handed out on authentication.
.TP
.B disable \fIvlan\fR
Disable a VLAN without deleting it.
.TP
.B rename \fIvlan\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename a VLAN. Existing assignments are kept, because they reference the
VLAN by UUID.
.TP
.B move \fR[\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR] \fIvlan\fR \fIunit\fR
Move VLAN to a different unit.
.TP
.B touch \fIvlan\fR
Re-index the object to fix potential index problems.
.SS Members
.TP
.B add_token \fIvlan\fR \fIuser\fB/\fItoken\fR
Add a token to the VLAN. Refused if the token is already a member of another
VLAN of this site.
.TP
.B remove_token \fIvlan\fR \fIuser\fB/\fItoken\fR
Remove a token from the VLAN.
.TP
.B add_role \fIvlan\fR \fIrole\fR
Add a role to the VLAN. Every token, host and device of the role is then in
the VLAN, and so is every role nested in it, unless a VLAN names one of them
more closely.
.TP
.B remove_role \fIvlan\fR \fIrole\fR
Remove a role from the VLAN.
.TP
.B add_host \fIvlan\fR \fIhost\fR
Add a host to the VLAN. Used for MAB port authentication.
.TP
.B remove_host \fIvlan\fR \fIhost\fR
Remove a host from the VLAN.
.TP
.B add_device \fIvlan\fR \fIdevice\fR
Add a device to the VLAN. Used for MAB port authentication.
.TP
.B remove_device \fIvlan\fR \fIdevice\fR
Remove a device from the VLAN.
.SS VLAN ID
.TP
.B vlan_id \fIvlan\fR \fR[\fIvlan_id\fR]
Set the 802.1Q VLAN ID (1-4094). Without \fIvlan_id\fR the VLAN ID is
removed and the VLAN name is sent to the switch instead.
.SS Object Changelog
.TP
.B changelog \fIvlan\fR
Show the object's changelog (chronological list of changes with author, timestamp and optional custom text passed via \fB\-\-changelog\fR).
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fIvlan\fR \fIchangelog_id\fR
Open the given changelog entry in the editor named by \fBEDITOR\fR to edit its custom text.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fIvlan\fR \fIchangelog_id\fR
Remove a single entry from the object's changelog.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fIvlan\fR
Clear the object's entire changelog.
.SS Policy Management
.TP
.B add_policy \fIvlan\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Attach a policy to the VLAN.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fIvlan\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Remove a policy from the VLAN.
.TP
.B list_policies \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIvlan\fR
List policies attached to the VLAN. Use \fB\-\-return\-type\fR to select the attribute returned (\fBname\fR, \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR, \fBuuid\fR) and \fB\-\-policy\-types\fR to filter by policy type.
.SS ACL Management
.TP
.B add_acl \fIvlan\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR
Add an access control entry. Use the \fBassign\fR ACL to allow assigning
this VLAN to other objects.
.TP
.B del_acl \fIvlan\fR \fIacl\fR
Remove an access control entry.
.TP
.B show_acls \fIvlan\fR
Display all ACLs for the VLAN.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fIvlan\fR
Enable ACL inheritance from parent objects.
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fIvlan\fR
Disable ACL inheritance.
.SS Configuration
.TP
.B config \fR[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-d\fR] \fIvlan\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set, append to or delete a config parameter.
.TP
.B get_config \fIvlan\fR \fIparameter\fR
Show a config parameter.
.TP
.B description \fIvlan\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set VLAN description.
.TP
.B info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIvlan\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set free-form info text. If \fIinfo\fR is omitted, the current info text is
opened in the editor specified by the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable.
.TP
.B dump_info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIvlan\fR
Dump the info text to stdout.
.SS Import/Export
.TP
.B export \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIPASS\fR] \fIvlan\fR
Export VLAN configuration.
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fIvlan\fR
Remove orphaned object references.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Display Options
.TP
.B \-a
Show all VLANs.
.TP
.B \-z \fISIZE\fR
Limit output size.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIFIELD1,FIELD2\fR
Display only specified fields.
.TP
.B \-\-policy\-limit \fIN\fR
Limit number of policies shown.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
Limit number of items shown per object.
.TP
.B \-\-sort\-by \fIFIELD\fR
Sort output by field.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse sort order.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Output without headers/borders.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Output as CSV.
.TP
.B \-\-csv\-sep \fISEP\fR
CSV separator character.
.SS General Options
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-acls
Preserve ACLs when moving VLAN.
.TP
.B \-\-password \fIPASS\fR
Password for encrypting exports.
.PP
Global options are available for all commands. See
.BR otpme (1)
for details.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B otpme-vlan add guests 100
Create a VLAN with VLAN ID 100
.TP
.B otpme-vlan add printers
Create a VLAN without a VLAN ID. The name "printers" is sent to the switch.
.TP
.B otpme-vlan vlan_id guests 110
Change the VLAN ID
.TP
.B otpme-vlan add_acl guests role netadmins assign
Allow the role "netadmins" to assign this VLAN
.TP
.B otpme-vlan add_role guests employees
Put every token, host and device of the role "employees" into the VLAN
.TP
.B otpme-vlan add_token servers joe/login
Put a single token into another VLAN, no matter which VLAN its roles are in
.TP
.B otpme-vlan add_role servers admins
Give a role nested in "employees" a VLAN of its own. Its members get it
instead of the VLAN of "employees", which stays with everyone else
.TP
.B otpme-vlan add_host printers hp\-lj\-01
Put a host into the VLAN, for MAB port authentication
.TP
.B otpme-token config joe/login vlans guests
Assign the VLAN to a token
.TP
.B otpme-host config myhost vlans berlin/guests
Assign a VLAN of another site to a host
.TP
.B otpme-site config berlin vlan_trusts master:berlin/guests
Allow objects of the site "master" to get the VLAN "guests" of berlin
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/otpme/
OTPme data directory
